# 8. Practical Modding Rules

## Rule 1

Do not think of animals as just datablocks.

The recovered system strongly suggests animals are:

- typed
- AI-driven
- spawn-pattern-driven
- post-death-interactive
- tightly linked to player ability systems

## Rule 2

Do not break AnimalData preload.

If type lookup fails, everything later becomes secondary error noise.

## Rule 3

Do not ignore nav mesh requirements.

At least part of the spawn logic depends on navigation support being active.

## Rule 4

Do not change attack-capable animals casually.

Attack logic appears to rely on configured combat state, and invalid attack setup can leave animals in broken behavior states.

## Rule 5

Do not treat corpse creation as cosmetic only.

Corpse generation appears to be a core bridge into skinning and slaughter systems.

## Rule 6

Player-facing animal abilities depend heavily on normal player runtime validity.

If connection, player, inventory, or skills are invalid, animal interaction abilities are likely to fail before any animal-specific logic matters.

## Rule 7

If you mod spawn ecology, focus on SpawnControl and pattern data first.

If you mod individual behavior, focus on AI nodes and behavior-tree selection.

If you mod post-kill economy, focus on skinning and slaughter logic.

If you mod taming, focus on type lookup, inventory integration, and item conversion.

